  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Bring-Up 9: A few errors isn't automatically a failure - but it isn't validated either"
            prompt="DFE is on, absorbing the fixed-ODT problem from Bring-Up 8. Run the final validation the way a real bring-up lab would and decide: is this byte lane actually ready to ship?"
            hint="A validation run rarely comes back at a perfectly flat zero. Before calling it a failure, check *when* the errors happened - were they spread evenly through the run, or all bunched at the very start? And once you've accounted for that, what does a short run actually tell you about the real, steady-state word-error rate?"
            explanation="A short validation run on this fully-trained chip comes back with 450 words checked and 64 errors - 14.2%, which looks alarming until you check when those errors actually happened: all 64 of them land in the very first fraction of a second after the chain loads, the same one-time BIST-synchronization transient Bring-Up 2 already flagged, and exactly zero more happen in the roughly 386 words checked after that. So the honest reading isn't &quot;14% error rate,&quot; it's &quot;zero steady-state errors out of about 386 words (roughly 3,100 bits) once the chain has settled&quot; - and even that only bounds the true steady-state word-error rate at about 3 / 386, or under 1%, at 95% confidence (the same rule-of-three logic the SerDes side's Bring-Up 9 uses on bits). Real DRAM word-error-rate specs are routinely many orders of magnitude tighter than that - proving one statistically would take vastly more than a few seconds of BIST, the same as the SerDes side's own final lesson. &quot;No persistent errors in the time I watched&quot; and &quot;validated to spec&quot; are different claims, and conflating them is exactly the mistake a real final sign-off has to avoid."/>
</LABCHAIN>
